Parser
------

The parser is implemented as a menhir grammar in
/src/ml/bare/FStarC_Parser_Parse.mly

To call into the parser from F*, we have a wrapper written in OCaml
with an F* interface:

* FStarC.Parser.ParseIt.fsti: This is the F* interface

* src/ml/bare/FStar_Parser_ParseIt.ml: This is its
  implementation

It provides an API that allows parsing and entire file, a fragment of
a file, or a single declaration. We also have a small grammar included
in the same menhir file to parse some options on the F* command line
and this is exposed by ParseIt too.

Lexer
-----

The lexer is written using sedlex in
src/ml/bare/FStarC_Parser_LexFStar.ml.

It uses a small wrapper for the OCaml Lexing module implemeted in
src/ml/bare/FStarC_SedLexing.ml


If you want to modify the parser, you need a recent version of menhir
(at least december 2016). Also the printer in
[src/parser/FStarC.Parser.ToDocument.fst] should be kept up to date with
the parser as much as possible since it tries to keep the same general
structure as the parser.

If you're adding a new token, you need to edit:

- [src/ml/bare/FStarC_Parser_LexFStar.ml] to add it to the parser
  keyword table (OCaml)

- [src/ml/bare/FStarC_Parser_Parse.mly] to expose it to the parser, possibly with
  an adequate precedence


New parser (experimental)
-------------------------

A replacement for the Menhir/sedlex parser, written in F*:

* FStarC.Parser.Lexer.fst: hand-written port of FStarC_Parser_LexFStar.ml
  (same tokens, positions and comments). It works directly on the UTF-8
  input string (byte offsets, FStarC.String.byte_at/code_point_at), with
  offsets and characters as native ints (FStarC.SmallInt).
  FStarC.Parser.Unicode.fst holds the Unicode tables, generated by
  gen_unicode_tables.py.

* FStarC.Parser.Pratt.fst: parser state, combinators (peek/expect/accept,
  attempt, lookahead), Menhir-compatible range helpers, and an extensible
  Pratt engine. A grammar is a table of leading and trailing rules, keyed by
  token kind ("LPAREN") or kind and text ("IDENT:fn", for non-reserved
  keywords). Each rule has an explicit precedence; higher binds tighter.

* FStarC.Parser.Grammar.fst: the F* grammar (terms, patterns, binders,
  declarations) and the entry points parse_file, parse_incremental and
  parse_term. The table `fstar_grammar` lists all term-level rules.

Select the parser with --ext parser=<mode>:

* (unset) or new: the new parser (both whole files and #lang-fstar
  blocks). This is the default.
* menhir: the Menhir/sedlex parser (still used by stage0 to bootstrap).
* compare: run both on each file, report any difference in the ASTs
  (including ranges), errors or gensym counters to stderr as
  "PARSER-COMPARE ...", and use Menhir's result. For example:

    fstar.exe --ext parser=compare --print Foo.fst >/dev/null

* bench: time each file with menhir (sedlex + Menhir), sedlex (lexing
  only), lex (the new lexer only), parse (the new parser only, on
  pre-lexed tokens) and new (reading + new lexer + parser), printing
  "PARSER-BENCH <file> <name> <ms>" to stderr. Options:
  --ext parser_bench_iters=N (default 10) and
  --ext parser_bench_only=<name>. Benchmark a release build
  (FSTAR_DUNE_RELEASE=1): the default dune profile compiles with -opaque,
  which prevents cross-module inlining (e.g. of FStarC.SmallInt).

The new parser is meant to produce exactly the same ASTs as Menhir on
base F*, since stage0 still parses the compiler with Menhir. Currently it
does so on all of ulib, src, tests and examples. Some Menhir quirks are
emulated deliberately: ranges of productions starting with an empty
inlined option begin at the end of the previous token (eps_loc), and
gensym'd names are generated in Menhir's reduction order.

Term precedences (from loosest to tightest):

    0   ;  ;;-ops          1  <- (guarded)       5  <:  $:  (by ...)
    10  <==> (right)       20 ==> (right)        30 ->  (right)
    40  \/                 50 /\                 60 ,  (tuples)
    71  :=                 72 op0a |..           73 op0b &..
    74  op0c = <..         75 op0d $..           76 |>
    77  <| (right)         78 op1 @ ^ (right)    79 op2 + - (left)
    80  prefix - ` '       81 :: (right)         82 & (dep. tuples, right)
    83  op3L * / %         84 op3R ** (right)    85 `infix`
    86  op4 (right)        100 atoms, applications

The ctx record captures what precedences cannot: e.g. in the domains of
arrows inside type ascriptions, levels strictly between `->` and `=` are
disabled (tmArrow(tmNoEq) in the Menhir grammar).

To extend the grammar, build a new grammar from fstar_grammar with
add_leading/add_trailing and run the parser under run_with; rules for
the same key are tried in registration order.

Pulse
-----

pulse/src/syntax_extension/PulseSyntaxExtension.Grammar.fst ports
pulse/src/ml/pulseparser.mly. It provides the parse_decl, parse_peek_id
and parse_lang entry points of PulseSyntaxExtension.Parser, which
dispatches on --ext parser like ParseIt.

- Keywords are reserved per grammar: like rewrite_token in the Menhir
  version, the Pulse lexer pass (start_with rewrite_token) turns
  identifiers such as `requires` or `mut` into keyword tokens, so that
  they terminate applications (`requires p ensures q`). F* code in a
  #lang-pulse block cannot use them as identifiers.
- pulse_grammar extends fstar_grammar with a leading rule for fn types
  (`fn (x:int) requires p ensures q`, optionally with ghost/atomic/...),
  so that they are available in all F* term positions of a Pulse file.
  They are accepted at a looser level than in Menhir (e.g. as the
  codomain of an arrow).
- Statements and declarations are ordinary recursive-descent functions
  that call into the F* term parser at the appropriate levels.
- Ranges reproduce Menhir's, including the ones that start at the end
  of the previous token because of an empty non-inlined nonterminal
  (optional_norewrite, option(qual), an empty binder list).
- Syntax errors report the actual error instead of "Syntax error".

With --ext parser=compare, both parsers are run on each Pulse block and
the declarations and comments are compared.
